            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

During the blower door test, we identified that your house has some air leakage associated with the following locations:



** The outside doors - these doors are not sealing properly (can seal with weatherstripping).

** From the older wood windows frames - these windows appers to have pink fibreglass insulation, which is a good insulator but a poor air sealer. I recommend removing fibreglass insulation and adding spray foam insulation whenever you are replacing those windows.

** Some of the electrical outlets in exterior walls of the house - e.g. the one near the entrance door. Foam wall plate gaskets can be installed behind the plates.



Savings: If you address the air leakage locations identified above, I estimate a modest 10% reduction in air leakage heat loss. This is estimated to save $ 80 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 2.3 GJ. There are currently no rebates available from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program for performing air sealing, however you will enjoy the annual energy savings every year.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT:



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant between $550 and $1000 is available for improving the airtightness of your home based on the level achieved. This is one of the most cost-effective energy-saving measures you can undertake. An air-sealing grant is available if the airtightness of your home is improved to achieve or exceed the air change rate target proposed above. Achieving any of the targets defined for your home typically requires that the work be performed by an air-sealing professional.



</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x6 with R20 batt insulation. I have modelled adding 2 inches (10) rigid foam polystyrene insulation to the exterior walls. 



Savings: By adding 2 inches(R10) rigid foam extruded polystrene insulation to these walls, you could potentially save $450 in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 13.5 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program ?????????insulation????????? rebate, a pre-approval is strongly recommended and my EnerGuide evaluation of your home satisfies the pre-approval recommendation. The rebate application, copies of sales receipts, and photos that clearly show where the insulation was added (and the thickness) must be received within three months of completing your insulation upgrade. For a full rebate, insulation products must be purchased in the Northwest Territories. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

https://aea.nt.ca/program/energy-efficient-products/

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT:



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of up to $5,000 is available for adding insulation to the exterior wall area of your home. Insulation grants are based on the percentage of wall area to which the insulation has been added and the amount of insulation added. A minimum of 20% of the exterior wall area of your home, excluding foundation walls, must be insulated in order to qualify for a grant.</mainWalls>
                <foundation>

The floor above your crawl space appears to be 2x12 joists with no insulation (R0). I have modelled adding 11 inches (R40) of fibreglass insulation below the joists. 



Savings: By adding 11 inches (R40 of fibreglass insulation below the joists of the floor above your crawlspace, you could potentially save $ 830 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 25 GJ, and qualify for up to a $ 1,000 rebate from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. All the plumbing, wiring penetrations must be properly sealed when you are adding the floor insulation to avoid any frozen pipes.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program ?????????insulation????????? rebate, a pre-approval is required and my EnerGuide evaluation of your home satisfies the pre-approval requirement. The rebate application, copies of sales receipts, and photos that clearly show where the insulation was added (and the thickness) must be received within three months of completing your insulation upgrade. For a full rebate, insulation products must be purchased in the Northwest Territories. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



Note: During my investigation, I did not notice any heating registers in the crawl space. If the crawl space is indeed heated, please disregard this recommendation.</foundation>
                <windows>

Most of the windows in this house (12 total) are older double pane wood windows, that are in need of an upgrade. I have modeled the energy savings associated with upgrading all of these older windows with ENERGY STAR?????? certified windows. In addition, I recommend full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.21 BTU per hour.



Savings: By replacing these older windows with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 250 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 7.5 GJ. There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, grants of either $125 or $250 per window rough opening are available when an ENERGY STAR certified or ENERGY STAR certified Most Efficient window is installed to replace an existing window. Replacing old, damaged or leaky windows with new ENERGY STAR certified products can help you save energy, improve comfort and reduce noise. To determine eligibility of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that it is included on the Searchable product list of eligible windows for the Canada Greener Homes Grant.



ENERGY STAR labels must be affixed to windows at the time of installation and remain in place until the time of your post-retrofit evaluation. If ENERGY STAR labels are removed prior to the post-retrofit evaluation, the following photos are your responsibility to acquire, retain and provide to the energy advisor at the time of the post-retrofit evaluation:

A photo of each newly-installed window showing a portion of the surrounding wall assembly from the inside, outside or both, AND



A photo of each ENERGY STAR label affixed to the window that clearly shows the ENERGY STAR certification mark, Manufacturer model code/number, U-factor/Energy Rating, NRCan reference number or a Certified Products Directory (CPD) number, and a Certified mark from one of the following:

????????? Canadian Standards Association (CSA)

????????? Intertek Canada

????????? Keystone Certifications

????????? Labtest Certification (LC)

????????? QAI Laboratories

????????? National Fenestration Council (NFRC)



Removed labels must be submitted and provided to the energy advisor.



Request your supplier/installer to include the following information on the invoice:

????????? Name of the homeowner and address of the house;

????????? Date of installation;

????????? Window make and model numbers;

????????? NRCan Reference Number;

????????? Indication of whether windows are ENERGY STAR or ENERGY STAR Most Efficient certified;

????????? Number of windows and location of each installation;

????????? U-factor/Energy Rating (ER);

????????? Itemized cost of each window and total cost of installation; and

????????? Itemization of any other work done as part of the installation, such as air/gap sealing and associated costs.



Please note that a window schedule (quote) cannot be used as a substitute for the aforementioned acceptable window validations and cannot be submitted to the portal as a standalone document to validate window installation and eligibility. However, the window schedule may be attached to the invoice provided there is a reference on the invoice to a schedule reference number to link the two documents.</windows>
                <VentilationSystem>

HRV



During my assessment, there appeared to be minimal ventilation for your home. Proper ventilation protects a home from unwanted toxins, pollutants, and odors and can also help protect the overall structure of a home or building by eliminating excess moisture from the air. An energy-efficient heat recovery ventilator is one of the best ways to control indoor air quality.



I recommend that you have an ENERGY STAR?????? rated Heat Recovery Ventilator (HRV) system, with a minimum 75% efficiency, designed and installed by an individual who holds current certification from an organization, such as HRAI.



Savings: By installing an ENERGY STAR?????? rated HRV, with a minimum 75% efficiency, your energy cost will increase by $ 120 per year and also increase your annual energy usage by 2.5 GJ. There are currently no rebates available for installing an HRV from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

</VentilationSystem>
                <HotWaterSystem>

You currently have a 182 litre electric hot water tank. Although electric hot water tanks are efficient from the perspective that 100% of the electricity used either heats the water inside or the heat radiating from the tank supplements the heating of the home, electricity is very costly. You may want to consider replacing your electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.95 or higher.



Savings: By replacing your current hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.95, could potentially save $ 1,200 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 2 GJ,  and qualify for up to $ 750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with a 83% efficient oil furnace. I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 97% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat.



Savings: By replacing your current propane furnace with a 97% AFUE condensing propane furnace, that incorporates an ECM fan blower, you could potentially save $ 1,250 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 22.5 GJ, 



By installing 97% AFUE condensing furnace with an ECM fan blower, you could qualify for up to $ 1,500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT:



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $1,600 is available for replacing an oil-fired furnace with an ENERGY STAR certified propane-fired furnace or boiler. To determine the eligibility of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that the unit is included on the Searchable product list of eligible furnaces or boilers for the Canada Greener Homes Grant.



Note: You can still get GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate if you were to install 96% AFUE condensing furnace but you will not be eligible for Canada Greener Homes Grant.</HeatingSystem>
                <WaterConservation>

Your house has two older 13 liter toilets that should be replaced with a lower water consumption models. When replacing toilets, purchase a 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model. Look for a MAP flush rating capacity of a minimum 800 - 100 grams per flush!



Savings: By replacing two older toilets with new 4.8 liter or less (or dual-flush) models, you could potentially save ~$100 per person, per year in water and energy savings. There are currently no rebates available for toilets from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

</WaterConservation>
                <AdditionalComments>

CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant, homeowners are eligible for up to $600 for the cost of pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and up to $5,000 total for the implementation of eligible retrofits completed after December 1, 2020.



Hiring a contractor is the recommended option in undertaking several of the home retrofits and for some retrofits it is a requirement. The contractor is responsible for complying with local bylaws and relevant provincial, territorial and federal legislation and guidelines.



ELIGIBILITY INFORMATION



For full details on eligibility requirements, eligible measures and grants for the recommended measures outlined below, please consult the Canada Greener Homes Grant website at canada.ca/greener-homes-grant or your Homeowner Information Kit.



Many eligible retrofit measures under the Canada Greener Homes Grant have certain conditions for eligibility. These include:

- In order to be eligible for reimbursement for your retrofit, you must complete at least one retrofit that is both eligible and recommended by an energy advisor in this Renovation Upgrade Report

- If you wish to undertake resiliency measures or purchase a thermostat, you must also undertake another eligible energy efficiency measure in order to qualify for a grant.

- Purchased equipment such as heat pumps and windows must meet eligibility criteria and must be on the eligible product list as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website. This information can also be found in the Homeowner Information Kit

- All mechanical and electrical systems, with the exception of thermostats, must be installed by a licensed and trained professional

- It is highly recommended that you use the attestation forms found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website and Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal (if applicable for your retrofit) confirming that:

--- installation of an electrical or mechanical system has been completed by a trained and licensed professional*

--- an earth-energy (ground or water) system was installed in accordance with CSA standards

--- the heat pump has been sized to your entire home

*When using a licensed professional it is highly recommended that you obtain proof of their licence to install equipment in your province or territory.

- All products must be purchased in Canada. Online purchases are only eligible if they are ordered from an online distributor located in Canada.



ADDITIONAL OPPORTUNITIES FOR INCENTIVES



You may access and combine grant funding from the Canada Greener Homes Grant with funding from other retrofit grant programs in your region. The sum of the funding you receive from all sources must not exceed 100% of the total cost of the pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and of each eligible retrofit measure.

Participants will be required to declare through the Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal funding or rebates received from other sources.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



LED Lights ????????? Hydro Community



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to fluorescent lighting. Fluorescent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Yellowknife, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year) from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test = Fail



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (kitchen range hood, bathroom fans and the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 5.5 Pascals. As this number is higher than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is expected to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current pellet stove vent through a wall and is susceptible to back drafting.



During my assessment of your home, I did see both smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors present. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed and operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has the following mechanical ventilation that vents to the outside of the house: 

        - two bathroom fans

        - a range hood

        - a clothes dryer



You may want to consider installing ENERGY STAR rated bathroom fans that seal well, use little electricity &amp; operate at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odors without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.

